cluding this one the in  goony branch of the southern Bantu tree  contains four of South Africa's official  languages Zulu Xhosa Swasey and in  dabeli the first two are the two most  spoken languages within South Africa as  a first language with Zulu representing  23% of the population and Xhosa  representing 16% of it the other two on  the other hand are spoken by only 2.5  percent in 2.1 percent respectively Zulu  is mainly spoken in kwazulu-natal and  most of em puma Lanka where Swasey and  underbelly are spoken as well Xhosa is  the majority language of the Eastern  Cape but has communities of speakers in  other provinces as well as other  official languages do the in goony  languages feature click consonants you  can hear one of them in the name of the  language Xhosa actually this is the  first time I've tried to pronounce a  click consonant so I may be pronouncing  the name of the language wrong from the  point of view of a native speaker but I  guess it's something like that Xhosa all  of the in goony languages are mute  intelligible to a large extent suited  SWANA the second largest of the southern  bantu branches is the suited SWANA  branch which has three languages within  it northern Sioux - representing 9.1% of  South Africa's population SWANA with 8%  and southern so - or simply Sioux - with  7.6% altogether they represent just  under a quarter of the population of  South Africa around thirteen point six  million people each of these three  languages serves as the majority  language in the province that it's most  widely spoken in southern suto in the  Free State SWANA in the northwest  province and there is also a significant  population of speakers in the northern  cape with which it shares a border and  northern Sioux - in Limpopo southern su  2 contains click consonants due to  contact with in goony languages while  some urban varieties of northern Sioux  to have in recent times been acquiring  clicks as well SWANA uses some click  sounds but only 